      "turns": [
        {
          "say": "What is the difference between gross pay and net pay?",
          "reply": "**Gross pay** is the total amount earned before any deductions. It's the starting figure for a pay period — built from your basic salary plus any allowances, overtime, or other earnings. **Net pay** is what actually reaches you after deductions are taken out. It's gross pay minus things like: - Income tax\n- Social insurance / employee contributions\n- Any other statutory or company deductions\n- Loan installments, if applicable So the simple relationship is: **Net pay = Gross pay − Total deductions** A few practical notes: - **Gross** is the figure used for things like salary comparisons, loan eligibility checks, and end-of-service calculations.\n- **Net** is the figure that matters for your day-to-day budget, since it's the amount deposited.\n- If your net looks lower than expected in a given month, the usual causes are a change in deductions (tax band, insurance, a loan installment starting) rather than a change in gross. If you'd like, I can walk you through how to read the gross and net lines on your own payslip in Nibras — just say the word.",
