      "turns": [
        {
          "say": "Explain how GOSI contributions are handled for employees.",
          "reply": "GOSI (General Organization for Social Insurance) contributions are handled as a payroll deduction and employer contribution, not as something you manage directly in Nibras. Here is the general picture: **How it works**\n- GOSI applies to eligible employees (typically Saudi nationals and, for some branches, non-Saudis for occupational hazards only). Eligibility depends on nationality and the applicable branch.\n- Contributions are split between the **employee share** (deducted from your pay) and the **employer share** (paid by GOFSCO on top of your salary).\n- The employee share is withheld automatically during the payroll run, so it appears as a deduction on your payslip rather than as a separate payment you make.\n- The employer share is a company cost and is not deducted from your pay. **The branches**\n- **Annuity (retirement/pension)** — the main branch for eligible nationals, with both employee and employer shares.\n- **Occupational Hazards** — covers work-related injury, with the employer paying the share.\n- **SANED (unemployment insurance)** — where applicable, again with an employer share. **Where you see it**\n- On your **payslip**, the employee portion shows as a GOSI deduction line.\n- Your **gross-to-net** calculation reflects it as a statutory deduction, alongside any other deductions. **What I can and cannot do here**\n- I can explain the structure and point you to where it appears on your payslip.\n- I cannot change, recalculate, or adjust GOSI amounts — those are set by GOSI rules and applied by payroll. If you believe a GOSI deduction on your payslip is wrong, raise it with HR/Payroll through **Agent** or **My**, and they can verify the contribution base and rate applied to your record. If you'd like, I can walk through how a GOSI line typically appears on a payslip, or you can check your own payslips in **My** to see the deduction as recorded for you.",
